// the branch the report deliberately does not take. a `die` at module scope leaves `main`'s block
// terminated - the abort runtime already called exit(1) - so there is no moment after the teardown left
// for a report to describe, and `[memory]` is absent rather than printed with a stale number
//
// it also holds an allocation when it stops, so the absence cannot be mistaken for "nothing to say"
ptr<int32> $p = mem::alloc<int32>(4);
echo mem::live_allocations();

die("stopping with memory still held");
